# Iluminate Player And Runtime Total Reengineering Plan

**Status:** monorepo implementation complete; production release gates remain
**Created:** 2026-10-07
**Scope:** lighting core, firmware partitura, browser runtime, GPU renderer,
Animate UX, expanded viewer, share viewer, video rendering, storage, privacy,
observability and cutover
**Replacement rule:** this document replaces the former Designer upgrade plan
in full. The completed geometry and fabrication work remains described by the
current source code and its authoritative handoff documents.
**Implementation rule:** there will be one new runtime and one coordinated
cutover. Do not add a production fallback, compatibility adapter, feature flag,
dual player, v1 reader or legacy rendering path.

**Implementation checkpoint — 2026-10-07:** the partitura.v2 core, prepared
runtime, Worker/controller, persistent WebGL2 player, single embedded/expanded
surface, immutable bundles, render-job schema, Chromium/FFmpeg worker,
private/review/unlisted/public policy flow and video-first public page are
implemented. Pixi and `/simulate-frame` were deleted. Core tests, 91 Designer
tests, dashboard production build, public-site typecheck/lint/build, worker
image build, Compose validation and local database migration pass.

The release is not complete: the external PlatformIO repository is not present
in this workspace, the production R2 bucket names/public media origin/internal
token are not configured, and therefore hardware parity plus an end-to-end
R2/video observation run remain open. A long-duration browser/GPU soak and the
concurrency load budgets also remain production release gates. YouTube
automation remains deliberately unimplemented; public publication still
requires explicit rights confirmation.

## 0. Executive Decision

Iluminate will replace the current request-driven preview with a compiled,
local and deterministic playback architecture.

The ESP32 partitura remains the principal executable product. The browser
player and the video renderer must execute the same behavioral contract; they
must not implement independent effect semantics.

The new system has four canonical outputs:

```text
AuthoringDocumentVNext
        |
        v
Iluminate compiler
        |
        +--> FirmwarePartituraV2   behavior shared by ESP32/web/video
        |
        +--> VisualSceneV1         geometry/material/camera only
                    |
                    v
              PlayerBundleV1
                 /       \
                v         v
       browser runtime   deterministic render worker
                              |
                              v
                       MP4 + poster image
```

Playback must never require one server request per frame. A viewer downloads an
immutable bundle once, prepares it once, and evaluates it locally. Public share
pages use a pre-rendered video by default and load the interactive runtime only
on explicit request.

The current player is replaced, not incrementally repaired. Pixi is removed
from the player runtime. React remains the application and control shell but is
not part of the frame loop. WebGL2 is the baseline renderer; its API boundary
must permit a future WebGPU backend without changing the core or UX.

## 1. Why A Total Replacement Is Required

The current architecture couples playback to application-server and React
work:

```text
requestAnimationFrame
-> POST /api/lighting/partituras/simulate-frame
-> validate and evaluate the complete partitura
-> serialize a full preview response
-> React setState
-> clear the Pixi stage
-> recreate scene objects, filters and render textures
-> render
```

The expanded viewer mounts another Pixi application while the embedded player
remains active. Pixi also runs its automatic ticker while the application calls
`render()` manually. Optical filters are recreated per preview frame and the
Face Graphic path does not currently destroy the backing source of its
per-frame render texture.

The present implementation therefore has four independent scaling problems:

1. Server work and network traffic grow with viewers multiplied by FPS.
2. React reconciles application state for data that belongs inside a player.
3. CPU and GPU resources are reconstructed instead of updated.
4. Expanded presentation duplicates the renderer and WebGL context.

At 30 FPS, one hundred viewers can produce up to 3,000 simulation requests per
second. One thousand viewers can produce up to 30,000. No optimization of the
existing endpoint makes that the correct public playback architecture.

## 2. Product Outcomes

The reengineering is successful only when all of the following are true:

- The ESP32 downloads a generated partitura and executes it without cloud
  access after download.
- The browser evaluates the same effect contract locally.
- The video renderer evaluates the same effect contract at deterministic time
  samples.
- `Play` generates no frame requests to the Iluminate server.
- React performs no render or state update for individual pixel frames.
- A player owns exactly one canvas and one GPU context.
- Opening the expanded viewer changes presentation, not renderer ownership.
- GPU buffers, shaders, masks and render targets are persistent.
- Memory and GPU-resource counts stabilize after scene load.
- Paused, hidden and offscreen players stop scheduling frames.
- Public playback defaults to an immutable video served by CDN.
- Interactive public playback downloads immutable artifacts from CDN and does
  not execute application-server simulation.
- Private client work is private by default and cannot become public without
  an explicit, authorized publication action.
- The visible Animate workflow remains familiar to current operators.

## 3. Non-Negotiable Decisions

### 3.1 One behavioral authority

`FirmwarePartituraV2` is the single declarative behavioral contract. Effects,
targets, scene clocks, clip activation, repetition, layer order, blend modes,
coordinate spaces and RGB quantization are defined once by lighting-core.

The browser and render worker may prepare optimized in-memory structures, but
those structures are derived from the partitura and cannot introduce new
semantics.

### 3.2 Firmware remains first-class

The partitura contains logical outputs and lighting behavior, never ESP32 pin
numbers, FastLED array names, WiFi credentials or device secrets. The firmware
owns physical pin mapping, power constraints, device state and hardware
drivers.

The controller continues to execute locally. Loss of Internet access after a
successful partitura download must not stop scene playback.

### 3.3 No legacy compatibility layer

- Do not normalize old authoring documents into the new document.
- Do not read both `partitura.v1` and `partitura.v2` in the new runtime.
- Do not retain the existing player as a fallback.
- Do not ship old and new playback loops behind a feature flag.
- Do not keep `/simulate-frame` as an alternative playback transport.
- Do not maintain duplicate effect implementations for comparison in
  production.

The one existing authoring document will be recreated or rewritten explicitly
for the new model during the coordinated cutover. No generic migration
framework is required for it.

### 3.4 No server in the frame loop

The server may validate, compile, publish, authorize, schedule video jobs and
serve metadata. It may not calculate ordinary interactive playback frames.

### 3.5 No React in the frame loop

React owns controls, panels, timeline editing and status. A controller object
owns clock, scheduling, worker communication, GPU updates and rendering.

### 3.6 One renderer

Embedded, expanded and interactive share presentations are layouts around one
player surface. They are not separate player instances for the same scene.

### 3.7 Video is derived, never authoritative

MP4, WebM, thumbnails and posters are immutable presentations of a published
bundle. They cannot be edited back into a partitura and cannot define device
behavior.

### 3.8 Privacy is explicit

Drafts and review renders are private. Public CDN objects and YouTube uploads
require a separate authorized publish action. Knowing how to generate a render
does not confer permission to publish it.

## 4. Scope And Non-Goals

### In scope

- New authoring-to-runtime compile boundary.
- `partitura.v2` contract and validator.
- Allocation-bounded compiled runtime.
- Shared deterministic effect library.
- Browser Worker playback.
- New WebGL2 optical renderer.
- React integration and preservation of the current Animate UX concepts.
- Expanded viewer using the same player instance.
- Firmware conformance contract and coordinated external-firmware update.
- Immutable player bundles.
- Private review, unlisted and public shares.
- Video rendering, R2 storage and Cloudflare CDN delivery.
- Optional user-authorized YouTube publishing.
- Performance, stress, parity and visual validation.
- Deletion of the replaced implementation.

### Not in scope

- Supporting old saved authoring documents.
- Supporting `partitura.v1` after cutover.
- Server-rendering every interactive frame.
- Streaming pixel frames over WebSocket.
- Making the player a collaborative editor.
- Real-time multi-user authoring.
- Bit-level electrical waveform simulation.
- Voltage-drop, power-injection or thermal simulation.
- A general-purpose 2D/3D rendering framework.
- WebGPU as a launch requirement.
- Automatic publication to the Iluminate or a customer YouTube channel.

## 5. Target System Architecture

```text
                         AUTHORING

React Designer/Animate UX
          |
          v
AuthoringDocumentVNext in browser memory
          |
          +------ Save ------> PostgreSQL document_json
          |
          +------ Compile ---> lighting-core compiler
                                  |
                                  +--> FirmwarePartituraV2
                                  +--> VisualSceneV1
                                  +--> diagnostics/checksums

                         LOCAL PREVIEW

FirmwarePartituraV2 + VisualSceneV1
          |
          v
PlayerBundleV1 loader
          |
          +--> Runtime Worker --> reusable RGB typed buffers
          |
          +--> WebGL2 renderer --> one canvas / persistent GPU resources

                         DEVICE

FirmwarePartituraV2
          |
          v
device download endpoint
          |
          v
ESP32 compiler/runtime --> FastLED/hardware outputs

                         PUBLISH

PlayerBundleV1 --content hash--> private or public R2
          |
          +--> interactive viewer through CDN
          |
          +--> render queue --> render worker --> MP4/poster --> R2/CDN
```

The core, browser and render worker are separated by data contracts. UI
components do not import effect implementations directly, and effect modules
do not import React, DOM, WebGL, database or HTTP code.

## 6. Canonical Models And Artifacts

### 6.1 AuthoringDocumentVNext

`document_json` remains the single editable database value, but its internal
schema may be replaced without compatibility fields or legacy normalizers.

It owns:

- Designer geometry and layer configuration.
- Controller and logical electrical topology.
- Pixel-density and compile settings.
- Zones, groups and targetable semantic objects.
- Scenes, tracks, clips and editable effect parameters.
- Optical treatments and Face Graphic configuration.
- Authoring camera and UX preferences that are intentionally persistent.

It does not own:

- Prepared runtime tables.
- GPU buffers or shader state.
- Current playback time.
- Per-frame RGB colors.
- Render jobs or video files.
- Device pins, credentials or active runtime state.

There will be one new schema version and one strict parser. Missing or unknown
required fields fail visibly; they do not receive historical defaults.

### 6.2 FirmwarePartituraV2

`partitura.v2` is generated, immutable and declarative. Its exact JSON schema
must be finalized before runtime implementation begins.

It includes:

- Contract and required core version.
- Project/partitura identity and content checksum.
- Logical outputs and output pixel counts.
- Stable pixel indices plus output/serial addressing.
- Spatial and normalized pixel coordinates required by effects.
- Resolved zone/group target membership tables.
- Scenes, tracks, clips, timing and loop rules.
- Effect identifiers and validated effect parameters.
- Blend and coordinate-space rules.
- Default scene.

It excludes:

- Authoring-only geometry.
- Paper/Pixi/WebGL objects.
- Optical materials and screen-space treatments.
- Camera or viewport state.
- Public-share settings.
- Device-specific hardware configuration.

Target membership should be generated as numeric pixel-index tables so the
firmware and browser do not repeatedly traverse string IDs while playing.
Human-readable IDs remain where they are operationally useful, but the hot path
uses compact numeric indices.

### 6.3 VisualSceneV1

The visual scene is derived from the same compiled authoring state and shares
the exact pixel indices used by `partitura.v2`.

It includes:

- Canvas and scene bounds.
- Pixel positions and display attributes.
- Static document/background geometry.
- Zone, channel and Face Graphic meshes or triangulation inputs.
- Optical treatment descriptors.
- Material and transmission parameters.
- Artwork references and checksums.
- Default camera, output aspect ratio and presentation presets.

It does not define scene clocks, clips, effects or RGB results.

### 6.4 PlayerBundleV1

A player bundle is an immutable manifest that binds compatible behavioral and
visual artifacts:

```text
PlayerBundleV1
├── manifest.json
├── partitura.v2.json
├── visual-scene.v1.bin
├── textures/*
└── checksums.json
```

The manifest records:

- Bundle version.
- Content hash.
- Partitura and visual-scene hashes.
- Required runtime version.
- Duration and available scenes.
- Default scene and camera.
- Asset URLs and byte sizes.
- Privacy/publication classification.

The bundle is content-addressed. Identical source content produces the same
hash and reuses the same compiled artifacts and video render.

### 6.5 RenderedMedia

Rendered media contains only derived delivery assets:

- MP4/H.264 baseline output.
- Optional WebM output when measured value justifies it.
- Poster image.
- Social/Open Graph image.
- Duration, dimensions, codec, bitrate and checksum metadata.

These objects are regenerated when the bundle hash or render profile changes.

## 7. Lighting-Core Reengineering

### 7.1 Required package boundaries

The new lighting-core remains pure TypeScript with no DOM, React, Pixi, WebGL,
Next.js, database or network imports.

Proposed structure:

```text
services/lighting-core/
├── contracts/
│   ├── authoring-vnext.ts
│   ├── partitura-v2.ts
│   ├── visual-scene-v1.ts
│   └── player-bundle-v1.ts
├── compiler/
│   ├── compile-authoring-document.ts
│   ├── compile-partitura-v2.ts
│   ├── compile-visual-scene.ts
│   └── diagnostics.ts
├── runtime/
│   ├── prepare-runtime.ts
│   ├── render-frame-into.ts
│   ├── runtime-clock.ts
│   └── typed-buffers.ts
├── effects/
│   ├── catalog.ts
│   ├── math.ts
│   └── implementations/*
├── transport/
│   └── ws2812b.ts
├── validation/
├── fixtures/
└── tests/
```

Names may change during implementation, but ownership may not collapse back
into the web application.

### 7.2 Compile once

The hot-path API is:

```ts
const prepared = preparePartituraRuntime(partituraV2);
const frame = createFrameBuffer(prepared.pixelCount);

renderFrameInto(prepared, sceneIndex, timeMs, frame);
```

`preparePartituraRuntime` performs once:

- Schema and semantic validation.
- Effect and parameter validation.
- Output and pixel-index validation.
- Target membership preparation.
- Scene/track/clip indexing.
- Clip sorting and schedule preparation.
- Coordinate normalization.
- Seed preparation for deterministic procedural effects.
- Allocation of reusable scratch and output buffers.

`renderFrameInto` may only:

- Resolve the scene clock.
- Determine active prepared clips.
- Evaluate effect math.
- Compose into an existing buffer.
- Quantize into the documented RGB representation.

It may not validate the partitura, sort arrays, create maps keyed by strings,
resolve groups recursively or allocate an output object for every pixel.

### 7.3 Typed runtime representation

Prepared runtime data uses typed arrays where practical:

- Pixel output and serial indices.
- Pixel X/Y and normalized coordinates.
- Target membership offsets and flat membership indices.
- Clip timing, layers, targets and effect indices.
- RGB frame buffers.
- Reusable blend/scratch buffers.

Strings and rich objects may remain in cold metadata, diagnostics and authoring
paths. They do not belong in the per-frame evaluation loop.

### 7.4 Deterministic effect contract

Each effect module declares:

- Stable effect ID.
- Parameter schema, defaults and bounds.
- Coordinate-space support.
- Deterministic seed rules.
- Numeric and RGB quantization rules.
- Firmware support/version requirement.
- Golden sample vectors.

No effect may read wall time, browser randomness, locale, DOM state or GPU
feedback. Time is always supplied explicitly.

Floating-point behavior that can differ between JavaScript and ESP32 must be
constrained by documented rounding points and tolerance. Effects that require
strict integer parity should use fixed-point or integer math in their canonical
definition.

### 7.5 Transport simulation is outside visual rendering

WS2812B-like byte order, transmit duration and maximum refresh estimates remain
in a transport module. The browser UI may display them, but the optical
renderer does not depend on them.

### 7.6 Core acceptance gates

- Every valid fixture compiles once and plays without further validation.
- Invalid contracts fail before a runtime is created.
- Repeated frame evaluation reuses all large buffers.
- A fixed input produces identical output across executions.
- Browser Worker and Node render worker pass the same golden vectors.
- Firmware passes the same vectors at required sample times.
- Runtime cost grows approximately linearly with active pixels and clips, not
  with total inactive authoring objects.

## 8. Firmware Contract And ESP32 Work

The external firmware project must be updated as a coordinated part of this
reengineering. It will consume only `partitura.v2` after cutover.

Required firmware responsibilities:

1. Download and authenticate the generated partitura payload.
2. Verify schema version, core version, byte length and checksum.
3. Reject unsupported effects or invalid output limits before activation.
4. Prepare output, target and clip tables once.
5. Allocate bounded runtime buffers before playback.
6. Execute the shared scene clock locally.
7. Render directly into the FastLED/output buffers.
8. Preserve logical-output-to-pin mapping in firmware configuration.
9. Store the last known good partitura atomically.
10. Recover after restart or network loss without cloud playback dependency.

Activation must be atomic: download to staging storage, validate and prepare,
then swap the active artifact. A failed download or compile leaves the previous
active artifact running.

Firmware parity fixtures must cover:

- Scene loop boundaries.
- Clip start/end boundaries.
- Every blend mode.
- Local, global and serial coordinate behavior.
- Empty outputs and unmapped pixels.
- Every effect at representative timestamps.
- RGB quantization and GRB transport order.
- Maximum supported pixel/clip counts.
- Invalid contract and unsupported-version rejection.

The monorepo remains the authority for the data contract and golden vectors;
the external firmware repository remains the authority for pins, drivers,
storage and hardware execution.

## 9. Browser Runtime

### 9.1 PlayerController

The React layer owns one long-lived `PlayerController` with an imperative API:

```ts
interface PlayerController {
  load(bundle: LoadedPlayerBundle): Promise<void>;
  play(): void;
  pause(): void;
  stop(): void;
  seek(timeMs: number): void;
  setScene(sceneId: string): void;
  setViewport(viewport: PlayerViewport): void;
  setPresentation(mode: "as_built" | "led_map"): void;
  setOpticalSettings(settings: OpticalPreviewSettings): void;
  resize(width: number, height: number, pixelRatio: number): void;
  destroy(): void;
}
```

State transitions are explicit:

```text
empty -> loading -> ready -> playing
                    |        |
                    v        v
                  paused <- seeking

any live state -> context-lost -> restoring -> previous live state
any live state -> destroyed
```

Calling methods in an invalid state produces a controlled diagnostic, not an
implicit second initialization.

### 9.2 Runtime Worker

The prepared partitura and effect evaluation live in a dedicated Web Worker.

Messages are coarse:

- `load`: send the partitura once.
- `ready`: prepared runtime metadata.
- `sample`: request the latest desired time when a buffer is available.
- `frame`: return a transferable RGB buffer and evaluated time.
- `dispose`: release runtime state.

The controller uses two or three transferable frame buffers. Only one sample
request may be in flight. If rendering falls behind, stale display times are
skipped rather than queued.

`SharedArrayBuffer` and cross-origin isolation are optional later
optimizations, not baseline requirements.

### 9.3 Clock and scheduling

- Use `performance.now()` as the monotonic browser clock.
- Scene time derives from a play start, offset and rate; it is not accumulated
  by adding nominal frame durations.
- `requestAnimationFrame` schedules visible presentation.
- The target frame rate is a policy, not a requirement to render duplicates.
- A hidden document, offscreen share embed or paused player schedules no
  ordinary frames.
- Seeking requests one sample and one render.
- Resizing rerenders the last available frame without reevaluating effects.
- React playhead display receives a throttled status signal, never RGB data.

### 9.4 Local bundle loading

The authenticated editor may build a bundle in browser memory after Compile.
A published/review viewer downloads an immutable bundle from object storage.

Loading has explicit stages and errors:

```text
manifest -> checksums -> partitura -> visual scene -> textures -> prepare -> GPU upload
```

The player must reject partial, incompatible or checksum-invalid bundles before
playback begins.

## 10. WebGL2 Renderer

### 10.1 Renderer boundary

The GPU renderer consumes only:

- A prepared visual scene.
- A reusable RGB frame buffer indexed exactly like the partitura.
- Viewport, presentation and optical settings.

It knows nothing about tracks, clips, effects, HTTP, React or database records.

```ts
interface PlayerRenderer {
  initialize(canvas: HTMLCanvasElement): Promise<void>;
  loadScene(scene: PreparedVisualScene): Promise<void>;
  updateColors(rgb: Uint8Array): void;
  updateViewport(viewport: PlayerViewport): void;
  updateOpticalSettings(settings: OpticalPreviewSettings): void;
  render(): void;
  resize(width: number, height: number, pixelRatio: number): void;
  destroy(): void;
}
```

### 10.2 One canvas and one context

The player creates exactly one WebGL2 context. Expanded mode changes the
surface container to a fixed full-screen layout or moves the existing host; it
does not instantiate another renderer.

Leaving Animate, unloading the bundle or destroying the component releases all
buffers, programs, textures, framebuffers, observers and event listeners.

### 10.3 Persistent GPU resources

Scene load creates and retains:

- Shader programs.
- Static vertex/index buffers.
- Instanced pixel geometry.
- Target and Face Graphic masks.
- Artwork textures.
- Optical ping-pong framebuffers.
- Color data texture or color buffer.
- Uniform blocks and treatment tables.

Frame render updates only:

- Current color data.
- Time-independent view uniforms when the camera changed.
- Treatment uniforms when calibration changed.
- Necessary framebuffer contents.

No shader, filter, framebuffer, mask texture, geometry object or text object is
created per animation frame.

### 10.4 Draw strategy

Direct LEDs use instanced geometry or a single batched mesh. Pixel colors are
looked up by stable pixel index from a GPU data texture or buffer.

Optical treatments are grouped into bounded passes:

1. Accumulate emitters into a reusable light buffer.
2. Apply target/source masks prepared from static geometry.
3. Apply separable blur with reusable ping-pong targets.
4. Composite rear, face and front layers in documented physical order.
5. Apply Face Graphic transmission using a persistent transmission texture.
6. Composite direct LEDs and optional outlines.

Treatment count must not create an unbounded number of full-resolution
framebuffers. Compatible treatments should share passes or atlases where
correctness permits.

### 10.5 Render area and culling

The logical Designer workspace may be larger than the display. The GPU renders
only the visible camera rectangle plus the maximum required optical padding.

```text
render bounds = camera bounds expanded by visible effect radius
```

Objects outside those bounds are excluded from CPU submission where useful and
clipped by the GPU. Public video renders an explicit output camera such as
1920x1080, not the entire authoring workspace.

Whole-viewport redraw is acceptable after the scene is persistent. Dirty
rectangles are not a launch requirement because overlapping blur and additive
composition make them complex and error-prone.

### 10.6 Resolution policy

- The final composite uses the visible canvas resolution.
- Device pixel ratio is capped by a quality profile and GPU limits.
- Blur/light accumulation defaults to half or quarter resolution when visual
  tests show equivalent output.
- Resize allocations are debounced and old targets are released immediately
  after the replacement is complete.
- Maximum texture and framebuffer sizes are checked before allocation.
- Quality may adapt downward after sustained missed frame budgets, but semantic
  colors and timing do not change.

### 10.7 Context loss

The renderer registers `webglcontextlost` and `webglcontextrestored` before
playback. On loss it stops frame submission, retains CPU-side prepared scene
data, reports one diagnostic and attempts a bounded resource rebuild after
restore.

Repeated restoration failure produces a visible player error with a video or
poster fallback when available. It must not silently create another context.

### 10.8 Renderer technology decision

Pixi is removed from the new player. Paper remains the Design authoring adapter
and is not used for Animate playback.

WebGL2 is chosen because it provides broad browser support, explicit resource
ownership, instancing, reusable framebuffers and shader control. The renderer
interface remains backend-neutral so WebGPU can be evaluated later from
measured need rather than introduced as a second launch implementation.

## 11. Animate And Viewer UX

The internal implementation changes completely, but the operator's mental
model remains similar.

### 11.1 Preserved concepts

- Design and Animate remain distinct modes.
- Compile remains explicit before Animate can trust changed physical topology.
- Scene, tracks, clips and timeline remain visible authoring concepts.
- Play, Pause, Stop, seek and scene selection remain available.
- `As built` and `LED map` remain presentation choices.
- Pan, zoom and target selection remain available in the embedded player.
- Lighting calibration remains contextual to selected zones/channels.
- Expanded viewing retains Return, scene identity, mapped pixel count and
  playback controls.
- Existing validation and stale-compile warnings remain conceptually present.

### 11.2 Intentional UX changes

- Expanded mode reuses the existing player surface.
- There is no second player modal or duplicate playback loop.
- `Rendering` refers to bundle preparation or video generation, not ordinary
  interactive playback.
- The UI may show a compact quality indicator when adaptive optical resolution
  is active.
- Publication is separated from preview through explicit Review/Public actions.
- Public/YouTube actions show privacy and rights confirmations.

### 11.3 React integration

React receives low-frequency player status only:

```ts
type PlayerStatus = {
  state: "loading" | "ready" | "playing" | "paused" | "error";
  sceneId: string;
  timeMs: number;
  durationMs: number;
  measuredFps?: number;
};
```

It does not store `animationPixels`, output rows or per-frame preview objects.
Timeline playhead updates should be visually smooth enough for the operator but
may be throttled independently of render FPS.

### 11.4 Editing while previewing

- Timeline/effect edits invalidate and rebuild the prepared behavioral runtime.
- Physical topology changes require Compile before playback resumes.
- Optical parameter changes update GPU uniforms without recompiling behavior.
- Static visual-geometry changes rebuild only affected visual resources.
- Rebuild happens outside the active frame callback and swaps atomically when
  ready.
- Playback may pause with a clear `Preparing scene` state when a structural
  rebuild cannot be made safely in place.

## 12. Share And Video Architecture

### 12.1 Publication flow

```text
Publish/Share action
-> validate permissions and current compile
-> create immutable bundle hash
-> upload bundle to appropriate R2 storage
-> coalesce render job by bundle hash + render profile
-> render deterministic frames
-> encode MP4 and poster
-> publish or authorize delivery
-> return stable Iluminate share URL
```

Viewing never starts a new render job unless an authorized publication changed
the source bundle or requested a new render profile.

### 12.2 Render worker

Video generation runs in a separate worker service, never inside the request
handler serving the editor.

The worker:

1. Claims a queued job with a lease.
2. Downloads and verifies the immutable bundle.
3. Loads the same core and WebGL renderer modules as the browser.
4. Uses a fixed output size, frame rate and deterministic time sequence.
5. Captures frames without real-time delay.
6. Encodes MP4/H.264 with FFmpeg.
7. Generates poster/social images.
8. Uploads outputs to R2.
9. Records checksums and marks the job complete.

Jobs are idempotent and content-addressed. Retries do not create duplicate
published media. Concurrency and CPU/GPU consumption are bounded by queue
policy.

### 12.3 Default public experience

The share page loads:

- Title, author and safe public metadata.
- Poster.
- MP4 video in loop with standard controls.
- Share/copy-link controls.
- Optional `Open interactive` action.

It does not load React Designer, Paper, authoring state or WebGL until the user
requests interactivity.

### 12.4 CDN and object storage

Use separate Cloudflare R2 buckets or equivalent hard-separated namespaces:

```text
iluminate-private-renders
iluminate-public-shares
```

The existing private artwork bucket must not be made public.

Recommended public paths:

```text
bundles/<bundle-hash>/manifest.json
bundles/<bundle-hash>/partitura.v2.json
bundles/<bundle-hash>/visual-scene.v1.bin
bundles/<bundle-hash>/textures/*
media/<bundle-hash>/<profile>/scene.mp4
media/<bundle-hash>/<profile>/poster.webp
```

Public immutable objects use long-lived cache headers and a custom CDN domain,
for example `media.iluminate.app`. Private objects require authenticated or
signed delivery and are never copied into the public bucket merely to make a
review link easier.

### 12.5 YouTube

YouTube is an explicit export destination, not the canonical share store.

- Never upload automatically.
- Use the customer's OAuth-authorized channel, not a global Iluminate channel.
- Require an authorized role and a final confirmation.
- Allow public, unlisted or private visibility where the API permits it.
- Record the initiating user, channel, video ID and resulting URL.
- Explain that public publication cannot guarantee removal of external copies.
- Support `Download MP4` even when YouTube integration is unavailable.

## 13. Privacy, Authorization And Share States

Every render/share has one explicit state:

| State | Audience | Delivery | Indexing |
|---|---|---|---|
| Private | Authorized organization members | Authenticated private object | No |
| Review | Possessor of opaque revocable link, optionally password/expiry protected | Signed or gated private object | No |
| Unlisted | Possessor of stable opaque link | Controlled delivery | `noindex` |
| Public | Anyone | Public R2/CDN | Allowed |

Rules:

- New content starts Private.
- Generating a video does not make it Public.
- Only authorized organization roles may change publication state.
- Organizations may disable Public and YouTube publication entirely.
- Review links support expiration and immediate revocation.
- Optional watermarking is part of the render profile for client review.
- Publication, revocation, download and YouTube actions are auditable.
- Public promotion requires an explicit rights/approval confirmation.
- Revocation deletes or disables origin access and purges controlled CDN
  caches, but the UI must state that downloaded or externally copied media
  cannot be recalled.

The public web container must not receive database credentials, R2-write
credentials, session secrets or device credentials. Public metadata and media
must be delivered through prebuilt manifests, read-only public objects or a
purpose-built restricted gateway.

## 14. Server APIs And Persistence

### 14.1 APIs retained or introduced

The target server surface performs coarse operations:

- Save AuthoringDocumentVNext.
- Compile and validate authoring state.
- Generate/publish `partitura.v2` for devices.
- Create immutable player bundle.
- Create/query/cancel render job.
- Create/revoke/update share policy.
- Request a signed private-review URL.
- Initiate user-authorized YouTube upload.
- Serve the device partitura artifact.

There is no ordinary frame-render endpoint.

### 14.2 APIs removed

- `/api/lighting/partituras/simulate-frame`.
- Any API returning per-frame output rows for browser playback.
- Duplicate modal-specific playback APIs or loops.

An explicit diagnostic endpoint may evaluate a requested frame for support or
test tooling only if it is authenticated, rate-limited and never used by the
player. It must call the same core runtime and must not become a fallback.

### 14.3 Persistence additions

Exact SQL belongs to implementation, but the model needs these responsibilities:

```text
render_artifacts
  id, client_id, bundle_hash, profile, status,
  private/public object keys, checksums, dimensions, duration, timestamps

render_jobs
  id, client_id, bundle_hash, profile, status,
  attempts, lease, error summary, timestamps

scene_shares
  id, client_id, partitura_id, artifact_id, opaque slug,
  access policy, expiry, revoked_at, created_by, timestamps

publication_audit
  actor, client, action, target, policy transition, timestamp, metadata
```

All mutable records are tenant-scoped. Content hashes deduplicate binary
artifacts without weakening authorization or exposing cross-tenant metadata.

`generated_json` will store the current `partitura.v2` device artifact. Old v1
payloads are cleared during cutover and are not accepted by the new device
endpoint.

## 15. Performance Contract

Performance is a product contract with automated gates, not a subjective smoke
test.

### 15.1 Per-viewer runtime gates

- Exactly one WebGL context.
- Zero network requests per animation frame.
- Zero React state updates per RGB frame.
- At most one Worker sample in flight.
- No unbounded queued frames.
- No creation of shaders, textures, framebuffers or geometry in steady-state
  playback.
- No growth in live GPU-resource counts after warm-up.
- JavaScript heap growth after warm-up remains within 5 MB over a 60-minute
  reference loop, excluding browser noise documented by the harness.
- Paused or hidden player has no active render loop.
- Context restoration does not create duplicate resources.

### 15.2 Frame budgets

Reference profiles must be defined with fixed fixtures. Initial targets:

| Profile | Output | Target | CPU preparation + submission p95 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Desktop standard | 1920x1080 @ 30 FPS | Full optical scene | <= 8 ms |
| Desktop high refresh | 1920x1080 @ 60 FPS | Supported scene profile | <= 12 ms |
| Mobile standard | viewport @ 30 FPS | Adaptive optical resolution | <= 20 ms |

GPU completion is measured separately where browser APIs permit it. A target is
not accepted solely because average FPS appears correct; p95/p99 stalls and
memory stability must be visible.

### 15.3 Core budgets

Benchmarks must report at least:

- Prepare time by pixel/clip count.
- Frame evaluation p50/p95/p99.
- Bytes allocated per evaluated frame.
- Peak runtime buffer size.
- Effect-specific cost.

Steady-state core evaluation should allocate zero large objects. Any unavoidable
small allocation must be measured, justified and bounded.

### 15.4 Scale budgets

- 1,000 concurrent public video viewers cause zero core frame evaluations on
  application servers.
- 1,000 concurrent interactive viewers cause zero core frame evaluations on
  application servers.
- Public static delivery is served from CDN/object storage.
- A single bundle hash creates at most one active render job per profile.
- Render-worker concurrency is explicitly capped and queue depth observable.
- Application origin remains responsive during render backlog.

## 16. Observability

### Browser metrics

Collect sampled, privacy-safe metrics:

- Bundle download and verification duration.
- Core prepare duration.
- GPU scene upload duration.
- First rendered frame.
- Frame CPU p50/p95/p99.
- Dropped/stale sample count.
- Adaptive quality transitions.
- WebGL context loss/restore/failure.
- Player state transition errors.
- Approximate heap usage only where the browser exposes it safely.

Do not send one telemetry event per frame. Aggregate locally and flush bounded
summaries.

### Server/worker metrics

- Bundle compile success/failure and duration.
- Artifact sizes.
- Render queue depth and age.
- Render duration, retry count and failure class.
- R2 upload/download failures.
- Publication transitions and authorization failures.
- CDN hit ratio and origin request volume where available.
- Device artifact download and rejection rates.

Logs must identify bundle hash, artifact/job ID and tenant-safe correlation ID,
never raw credentials or private scene contents.

## 17. Validation Strategy

### 17.1 Contract tests

- Strict schema acceptance/rejection for AuthoringDocumentVNext,
  FirmwarePartituraV2, VisualSceneV1 and PlayerBundleV1.
- Checksum and version failures.
- Unknown effects and invalid parameters.
- Target and pixel-index referential integrity.
- Output count and device-limit validation.

### 17.2 Core parity tests

Golden vectors have the form:

```text
partitura + sceneId + timeMs -> ordered RGB frame
```

They cover every effect, blend, coordinate space and boundary. The Node core,
browser Worker and firmware must consume the same input vectors.

Parity is against the new written contract and approved vectors, not against
accidental behavior of the old player.

### 17.3 Runtime stress tests

- Ten thousand sequential frames with stable buffer identities.
- Sixty-minute loop with heap/resource sampling.
- Rapid play/pause/seek/scene changes.
- Resize and DPR changes during playback.
- Tab hide/show and offscreen transitions.
- Worker delay and stale-frame skipping.
- Load/destroy cycles.
- Synthetic context loss and restoration.
- Maximum supported scene fixture.

### 17.4 Renderer visual tests

Approved images are captured for:

- Direct LED map.
- Front illumination by material.
- Rear halo and wall wash.
- Treatment ordering and overlap.
- Face Graphic opaque, clear and translucent transmission.
- Day/night backgrounds.
- Camera crop and optical padding.
- Half/quarter-resolution optical passes.
- Embedded and expanded layouts.

Visual tests use perceptual tolerances appropriate for GPU rasterization, with
separate semantic assertions for layer order and masks.

### 17.5 UX end-to-end tests

- Compile, enter Animate, play, pause, stop and seek.
- Change scenes and effect parameters.
- Select and calibrate one or multiple optical targets.
- Switch LED Map/As Built.
- Expand and return without creating a second context.
- Edit while paused and rebuild correctly.
- Detect stale physical compile.
- Create private review, revoke it and verify denial.
- Publish public video and open share page without editor code.
- Open interactive share and verify no frame API traffic.

### 17.6 Load tests

Load tests must distinguish:

- Application metadata endpoints.
- Private signed delivery.
- Public CDN delivery.
- Render-job creation.
- Render-worker throughput.

No load test should simulate public scale by repeatedly calling a frame API,
because that API does not exist in the target design.

### 17.7 Hardware acceptance

At least one real ESP32 installation must:

- Download `partitura.v2`.
- Verify and activate it atomically.
- Match golden RGB frames at selected timestamps.
- Loop for an extended period without cloud access.
- Recover the last known good artifact after restart.
- Reject a deliberately incompatible artifact without interrupting the active
  scene.

## 18. Implementation Workstreams

These are construction workstreams, not incremental production releases. The
new path is merged and deployed only when the complete cutover gate passes.

### Workstream A — Contract reset

Deliverables:

- Final AuthoringDocumentVNext schema.
- Final `partitura.v2` schema.
- Final VisualSceneV1 and PlayerBundleV1 contracts.
- Effect parameter contracts and deterministic numeric rules.
- Device limits and bundle limits.
- Golden-vector format.
- New default document containing the current scene reconstructed intentionally.

Exit gate:

- Contracts reviewed by web, core and firmware ownership.
- No unresolved semantic choice is delegated to renderer implementation.

### Workstream B — New lighting-core

Deliverables:

- Strict validators.
- Authoring compiler.
- Runtime preparation.
- Allocation-bounded `renderFrameInto`.
- Effect modules.
- WS2812B transport projection.
- Node/browser builds.
- Core benchmarks and golden tests.

Exit gate:

- All golden vectors pass.
- Performance and allocation budgets pass for reference fixtures.

### Workstream C — ESP32 runtime v2

Deliverables in the external firmware repository:

- V2 parser and verifier.
- Prepared runtime tables.
- Effect implementations/conformance.
- Atomic storage and activation.
- Device download/update flow.
- Hardware parity harness.

Exit gate:

- Hardware acceptance passes without a v1 reader compiled into the target
  firmware release.

### Workstream D — Browser Worker runtime

Deliverables:

- Bundle loader and checksum verification.
- Runtime Worker.
- Transferable frame-buffer pool.
- Clock and scheduler.
- PlayerController state machine.
- Visibility/offscreen suspension.
- Stress and lifecycle tests.

Exit gate:

- Local playback runs with network disabled after load.
- No React frame state and no server frame requests.

### Workstream E — WebGL2 renderer

Deliverables:

- Renderer resource manager.
- Static-scene preparation/triangulation.
- Instanced direct LED pass.
- Optical accumulation and reusable blur passes.
- Persistent target/Face Graphic masks.
- Camera/culling and resize policy.
- Context restoration.
- Visual/performance harness.

Exit gate:

- Visual baselines approved.
- Resource and frame budgets pass.
- Sixty-minute stress test remains stable.

### Workstream F — Animate UX integration

Deliverables:

- One player surface.
- Existing control concepts connected to PlayerController.
- Timeline status bridge without RGB React state.
- Embedded/expanded responsive layouts over the same surface.
- Optical calibration uniform/resource updates.
- Removal of duplicate modal/player loops.
- Operator end-to-end tests.

Exit gate:

- UX acceptance scenario passes.
- Opening expanded mode never changes context count.

### Workstream G — Publishing and media

Deliverables:

- Bundle artifact persistence.
- Private/public R2 separation.
- Render queue and worker.
- MP4/poster profiles.
- Share policy and audit model.
- Private review and public share pages.
- Optional interactive viewer.
- Download MP4.
- YouTube OAuth publication flow.

Exit gate:

- Public video and interactive viewers generate no frame-server load.
- Privacy transitions and revocation tests pass.

### Workstream H — Atomic cutover and deletion

Deliverables:

- Maintenance-window runbook.
- Backup of the current record for operational rollback only.
- Explicit recreation/rewrite of the one authoring document.
- `partitura.v2` generation and device publication.
- Coordinated firmware activation.
- Deletion of the old runtime, player and endpoint.
- Dependency and documentation cleanup.

Exit gate:

- Production has one core, one player and one firmware schema.
- No legacy runtime code is reachable or shipped.

## 19. Atomic Cutover Runbook

The implementation may be developed in an isolated branch or changeset, but it
is not exposed as a second production mode.

1. Freeze authoring and device publication for a defined maintenance window.
2. Back up the current database record and current firmware artifact solely for
   full-release rollback.
3. Confirm new web, core, render worker and firmware artifacts passed all gates.
4. Deploy the new services without enabling user traffic.
5. Recreate/rewrite the single authoring document in the VNext schema.
6. Compile and persist `partitura.v2` plus VisualSceneV1.
7. Activate firmware v2 and download the new artifact.
8. Run hardware, browser, expanded-viewer and private-share smoke tests.
9. Enable user traffic.
10. Observe defined health metrics through the acceptance window.

If the cutover fails, roll back the complete release and database snapshot. Do
not activate a compatibility switch inside the new release.

After acceptance:

- Delete old backups according to retention policy.
- Remove any temporary migration script.
- Do not retain a callable v1 conversion endpoint.

## 20. Required Deletion Inventory

The cutover is incomplete while equivalent legacy responsibilities remain.
Delete or replace:

- `services/web/iluminate/components/lighting/designer/designer-webgl-player.tsx`
- `services/web/iluminate/components/lighting/designer/rendering/designer-player-renderers.ts`
- `services/web/iluminate/components/lighting/designer/designer-playback-frame.ts`
- `/api/lighting/partituras/simulate-frame`
- Playback RAF/fetch loops in `partitura-workspace.tsx` and `PlayerModal`.
- React `animationPixels`/per-frame preview state as the player data path.
- The duplicate expanded `DesignerWebglPlayer` mount.
- Pixi player dependency and Pixi-specific resource handling.
- `partitura.v1` schemas, fixtures, validator/generator and production exports.
- Compatibility normalizers for the replaced authoring document.
- Any documentation claiming server frame simulation is the player path.

Historical facts may remain in Git history; they must not remain as production
fallback code.

## 21. Proposed Target File Ownership

```text
services/lighting-core/
  contracts/*
  compiler/*
  runtime/*
  effects/*
  transport/*
  fixtures/*
  tests/*

services/web/iluminate/components/lighting/player/
  player-controller.ts
  player-worker.ts
  player-worker-protocol.ts
  player-surface.tsx
  player-status.ts
  gpu/
    webgl2-renderer.ts
    resource-manager.ts
    scene-preparer.ts
    passes/*
    shaders/*

services/web/iluminate/lib/lighting/
  player-bundle-loader.ts
  player-bundle-builder.ts

services/render-worker/
  worker entrypoint
  queue adapter
  deterministic frame capture
  ffmpeg encoder
  R2 artifact adapter

services/firmware/
  contract notes and shared fixtures only

external iluminate-firmware-esp32 repository/
  v2 parser, prepared runtime, effects, storage and drivers
```

The public share surface must stay free of authoring and privileged storage
dependencies.

## 22. Acceptance Scenario

The complete system must pass this scenario without a legacy path:

1. Create or open the new-format partitura document.
2. Build physical geometry and compile the pixel map.
3. Create a scene with overlapping effects on zones/groups.
4. Enter Animate and load one local player bundle.
5. Play, pause, seek and change presentation with DevTools showing no frame
   requests.
6. Expand the viewer and confirm the same canvas/context continues playing.
7. Change optical calibration and confirm persistent resources update without
   rebuilding the behavioral runtime.
8. Loop for sixty minutes and pass heap/GPU stability budgets.
9. Generate `partitura.v2`, download it to the ESP32 and match selected golden
   timestamps on hardware.
10. Disconnect the controller from the network and confirm continuous local
    playback.
11. Create a private review render and confirm unauthenticated public access is
    denied.
12. Revoke the review link and confirm subsequent access is denied.
13. Explicitly publish a public version and load its MP4 page from CDN.
14. Open the optional interactive view and confirm playback remains local.
15. Simulate 1,000 public viewers and confirm no application-server frame
    evaluation occurs.
16. Remove the publication and purge controlled delivery.

## 23. Validation Commands And Harnesses

Existing commands remain useful while the new suites are introduced:

```bash
cd services/lighting-core
npm test

cd services/web/iluminate
npm test
npm run build
```

The reengineering must add stable commands equivalent to:

```bash
# Contract and deterministic core vectors
npm run test:contracts
npm run test:runtime
npm run test:parity

# Measured core/runtime performance
npm run bench:runtime
npm run test:runtime-stress

# Renderer correctness and stability
npm run test:player
npm run test:player-visual
npm run test:player-stress

# Product and scale
npm run test:e2e-player
npm run test:e2e-share
npm run test:load-share
```

Command names may follow repository conventions, but all these validation
categories are mandatory and must run without manual inspection except final
visual/hardware approval.

Firmware CI must build the target board configuration and run host/golden tests.
Hardware-in-loop validation remains a release gate even when it cannot execute
on every pull request.

## 24. Risks And Mitigations

### Custom renderer complexity

Risk: replacing Pixi with explicit WebGL2 increases implementation ownership.

Mitigation: keep the renderer narrowly specialized, use a small pass graph,
centralize resource ownership, require visual baselines and avoid building a
general scene framework.

### Browser/GPU variation

Risk: shader precision, limits and driver behavior vary.

Mitigation: conservative WebGL2 feature baseline, capability checks, explicit
limits, adaptive optical resolution, context recovery and tested video fallback.

### Firmware/browser numeric divergence

Risk: floating-point effects differ across JavaScript and ESP32.

Mitigation: documented rounding points, fixed-point math where necessary and
shared golden vectors at boundary timestamps.

### Worker scheduling and transfer stalls

Risk: frames arrive late under main-thread or device pressure.

Mitigation: bounded buffer pool, one request in flight, stale-frame skipping,
monotonic clock and adaptive presentation FPS.

### GPU memory regression

Risk: static masks and optical targets consume excessive VRAM.

Mitigation: explicit resource ledger, bounded render-target pool, resolution
profiles, scene limits and repeated load/destroy stress tests.

### Video/browser visual mismatch

Risk: headless capture differs from interactive output.

Mitigation: same bundle, core, renderer and shaders; fixed render profile;
cross-capture visual regression suite.

### Render backlog

Risk: many publications saturate the render worker.

Mitigation: hash deduplication, bounded concurrency, queue backpressure,
priority policy, progress UX and horizontally replaceable workers.

### Client confidentiality

Risk: review content becomes publicly cacheable or is uploaded externally.

Mitigation: separate private/public storage, explicit policy transitions,
authorization, audit, expiry, watermarking and no automatic YouTube publishing.

### Big-bang cutover

Risk: no compatibility mode reduces incremental production recovery options.

Mitigation: complete preproduction environment, full automated gates, explicit
maintenance window, whole-release rollback and the intentionally small number
of existing authoring documents.

## 25. Documentation Updates At Implementation

After this plan is approved and implementation changes behavior, update the
authoritative sources in the same work:

- `.agent/DESIGNER_HANDOFF.md`
- `.agent/DESIGNER_UX_CONTRACT.md`
- `.agent/EFFECT_TARGETING_MODEL.md` if target/runtime semantics change
- `.agent/DATABASE_MODEL.md`
- `.agent/PUBLIC_SITE_DIRECTION.md`
- `docs/partitura-lifecycle.md`
- `docs/production-deployment.md`
- `services/lighting-core/README.md`
- `services/firmware/README.md`
- `services/web/iluminate-prompt-builder/catalog.mjs`
- Public/share and render-worker operational runbooks

Do not update those documents speculatively before a corresponding decision is
approved or implemented. Once implemented, no authoritative document may still
describe `/simulate-frame`, Pixi reconstruction or a duplicate expanded player
as the supported path.

## 26. Architecture Review Checklist

The plan is ready to implement when reviewers explicitly accept:

- [x] `partitura.v2` is the only post-cutover firmware/runtime behavior contract.
- [x] The existing authoring document does not require backward compatibility.
- [x] Browser playback is completely local after bundle load.
- [x] React does not receive RGB frames.
- [x] Pixi is removed from the player and WebGL2 is the baseline backend.
- [x] Embedded and expanded presentations share one player instance.
- [x] Optical rendering uses persistent resources and bounded passes.
- [x] Video is the default public-share experience.
- [ ] Interactive public viewing is optional and CDN-loaded.
- [x] Private/review/public states and bucket separation are mandatory.
- [x] YouTube publication is explicit and customer-authorized.
- [ ] Firmware work and hardware parity are part of the same release gate.
- [x] No compatibility adapter, production fallback or dual runtime will ship.
- [x] The atomic cutover and whole-release rollback strategy are acceptable.
- [x] Performance budgets are release blockers, not advisory metrics.

## 27. Definition Of Done

This reengineering is complete only when:

1. `partitura.v2` is generated, validated, downloaded and executed by the
   production ESP32 runtime.
2. Browser and firmware pass the same approved golden vectors.
3. The browser player evaluates locally with no per-frame network traffic.
4. The player uses one WebGL2 context and persistent GPU resources.
5. Embedded and expanded UX use the same player instance.
6. Memory, frame and lifecycle performance gates pass.
7. Private review and public video sharing operate through separated storage.
8. The public page serves video from CDN and does not load the editor.
9. Optional interactive sharing runs locally after an immutable bundle download.
10. The render worker produces deterministic MP4/poster artifacts by bundle hash.
11. The old player, endpoint, loops, v1 contract and compatibility code are
    deleted.
12. Authoritative documentation describes only the new architecture.
13. Production passes the full acceptance scenario and observation window.

Until all thirteen conditions are met, the project is in reengineering and not
finished.
